@repo/metering

Portions of the behaviour in this package are derived from Autumn
(https://github.com/useautumn/autumn), which is licensed under the Apache
License, Version 2.0.

No source files were copied. What was taken is the semantics — read from their
implementation and reimplemented here — specifically:

  - the shape of a metered entitlement: an allowance that refills on an
    interval, drawn down per request;
  - reset intervals expressed as an interval plus a count, so that "quarterly"
    is `month` x 3 rather than a separate kind of thing;
  - all-or-nothing draw-down for a batched request.

Where this package deliberately DIFFERS from Autumn, and why:

  1. Reset windows are computed from a fixed anchor rather than stored in a
     `next_reset_at` column and advanced by a cron.

     Autumn's server/src/cron/resetCron/resetCustomerEntitlement.ts advances by
     exactly one interval per run regardless of how overdue the row is, so a
     cron outage spanning several periods restores one period of allowance and
     leaves the stored boundary in the past.

  2. Month-based intervals preserve the anchor day across short months.

     Autumn's shared/utils/billingUtils/intervalUtils/intervalArithmetic.ts
     documents `addInterval` as "preserves the anchor day (Stripe-compatible
     end-of-month behavior)", computes `anchorDay` and `isMonthBased`, and then
     uses neither -- it applies date-fns `addMonths` to the previous boundary.
     Because `addMonths` clamps, repeated stepping walks the date backwards
     permanently: 31 Jan -> 28 Feb -> 28 Mar. Deriving each boundary from the
     original anchor keeps the clamp local to the short month.

  3. Concurrency safety is not implemented here.

     Autumn achieves atomic draw-down with Redis Lua scripts
     (server/src/_luaScriptsV2/fullSubjectDeduction/). i10 runs this inside a
     single-threaded Durable Object per (tenant, feature), so the atomicity is
     a property of where the code runs rather than of the code. This package is
     pure arithmetic and makes no concurrency claim of its own.
